    {
      "entryId":"ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0067","sequence":79,
      "meaning":"Smiling, Nārada speaks gently yet enigmatically: their daughter is a mine of every virtue—naturally beautiful, gracious and wise, named Umā, Ambikā and Bhavānī. Endowed with every auspicious mark, she will remain forever dear to her beloved; her marital felicity will be unfailing, and her parents will gain fame through her. The whole world will worship her, nothing will be unattainable to those who serve her, and women remembering her name will ascend the razor’s edge of wifely fidelity. Yet, after these good signs, he names a few apparent faults: her husband will be qualityless, honorless, parentless, detached and free of every doubt—a matted-haired yogī with desireless mind, naked and in inauspicious attire. Her palm bears the line that such a lord will meet her.",
