    {
      "entryId":"ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0069","sequence":81,
      "meaning":"Nārada nevertheless offers one remedy, if providence will assist: Umā will certainly obtain a husband exactly as he described. He recognizes every listed fault in Śiva, but if she marries Śaṅkara, everyone will call those faults equivalent to virtues. Wise people do not blame Hari for reclining upon a serpent-bed; the sun and fire consume every kind of substance, yet no one calls them bad. Every pure and impure stream flows into the Ganges, yet no one calls her polluted. No fault attaches to the powerful Lord—like the sun, fire and Ganges. But if dull humans, proudly trusting their discrimination, imitate such conduct, they fall into hell for an aeon: can an individual creature equal the Lord?",
